## Proof

Two projections $p, q \in B(H)$ are **equivalent** (in the Murray–von Neumann sense) if there exists a partial isometry $v \in B(H)$ such that

$$v^*v = p, \qquad vv^* = q.$$

Recall that for any partial isometry $v$, the operators $v^*v$ and $vv^*$ are projections, called the **initial projection** and the **final projection** of $v$. The key property of a partial isometry is:

**$v$ is an isometry on the initial space, and zero on its orthogonal complement.** Concretely, $v^*v = p$ means $v$ acts isometrically on $p(H)$ and annihilates $(p(H))^\perp = (1-p)(H)$; likewise $vv^* = q$ means $v^*$ acts isometrically on $q(H)$ and annihilates $(q(H))^\perp$.

We claim $v$ restricts to a **unitary** (surjective isometry) from $p(H)$ onto $q(H)$.

**$v$ maps $p(H)$ into $q(H)$.** For any $\xi \in p(H)$, we have $p\xi = \xi$, so
$$q(v\xi) = vv^*v\xi = v(v^*v)\xi = v(p\xi) = v\xi,$$
hence $v\xi \in q(H)$. Thus $v\big|_{p(H)} : p(H) \to q(H)$.

**$v\big|_{p(H)}$ is an isometry.** For $\xi \in p(H)$,
$$\|v\xi\|^2 = \langle v\xi, v\xi\rangle = \langle v^*v\,\xi, \xi\rangle = \langle p\xi,\xi\rangle = \langle \xi,\xi\rangle = \|\xi\|^2.$$

**$v\big|_{p(H)}$ is surjective onto $q(H)$.** For any $\eta \in q(H)$, we have $q\eta = \eta$, i.e. $vv^*\eta = \eta$, so $\eta = v(v^*\eta)$. Moreover $v^*\eta \in p(H)$ since
$$p(v^*\eta) = v^*v\,v^*\eta = v^*(vv^*\eta) = v^*(q\eta) = v^*\eta.$$
Thus every $\eta \in q(H)$ is $v$ of some element of $p(H)$, namely $v^*\eta$.

Therefore $v\big|_{p(H)} : p(H) \to q(H)$ is a surjective linear isometry, i.e. a unitary between the two Hilbert spaces $p(H)$ and $q(H)$.

A unitary between two Hilbert spaces preserves every Hilbert-space invariant, in particular the **Hilbert dimension** (cardinality of an orthonormal basis). Hence

$$\dim p(H) = \dim q(H).$$

